WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.630 --> 00:00:02.520
MINHAZ KAZI: Welcome
to "DataVis DevTalk."

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.040
I'm your host, Minhaz
Kazi, developer advocate

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.690
for Data Studio.

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.790
In this episode of
"DataVis DevTalk,"

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:12.120
you will learn how to manage
versions of your connector code

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.150
and how to use separate
deployments for your connector.

00:00:15.150 --> 00:00:16.486
Let's get started.

00:00:16.486 --> 00:00:22.090
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:24.480
As you develop code for
your Community Connector,

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:27.840
you can test and use your
connector in Data Studio

00:00:27.840 --> 00:00:30.180
by deploying it
from Apps Script.

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:33.630
Deployments let Data Studio
execute the connector code

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.220
by Apps Script.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:38.880
You can access and manage all
deployments for your connector

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:42.060
by going to the Publish
menu and selecting Deploy

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:45.570
From Manifest in the
Apps Scripts environment.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:47.370
After selecting
this option, you'll

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:51.390
see the list of all available
deployments for your connector.

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:53.130
For a new connector
project, you'll

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:55.380
start with the head deployment.

00:00:55.380 --> 00:00:58.170
This head deployment
will always be associated

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:00.630
with the current or latest
version of your code

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.580
that you're working with.

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:06.030
Click on any deployment to
reveal the deployment link.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:10.650
You or your users can use this
link to directly access and use

00:01:10.650 --> 00:01:13.230
your connector in Data Studio.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:15.000
If you go to your
deployment link,

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.020
it will open up your Data Studio
with your connector selected.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:21.690
If this is the first time
you're using a connector,

00:01:21.690 --> 00:01:23.520
Data Studio will ask
for authorization

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:25.090
for that connector.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:27.900
This is where you can start
testing your connector.

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:29.520
The other way to
test your connector

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:32.220
is to view the deployment
list in Apps Script

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:34.560
and get the idea for deployment.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.900
Next, open up Data
Studio in a browser tab

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.640
and go to data sources.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:41.520
Start adding a new data source
and click on the developers

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.590
icon on top right.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:45.210
This will give you
an interface where

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:48.540
you can enter the connector
ID, validate the deployment,

00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:51.960
and then select the
connector to start using it.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:54.600
This method is helpful to
evaluate how your connector

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.140
card might look like in the
Data Studio connector gallery.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.810
I mentioned earlier that the
head deployment is always

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:04.180
associated with the latest
version of your connector code.

00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:07.530
Apps Script lets you save
different versions or snapshots

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:08.910
of your code.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:10.889
These snapshots are
numbered sequentially,

00:02:10.889 --> 00:02:13.650
and you can add your own
notes to each version

00:02:13.650 --> 00:02:15.970
as you make the changes.

00:02:15.970 --> 00:02:17.850
Let's say you're
developing a connector

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.580
and you want a few
colleagues to try it out.

00:02:20.580 --> 00:02:22.200
However, at the
same time, you want

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.360
to keep working on the code.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:26.370
So you save a new
version of your code,

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:29.970
we'll call it Test Candidate
1, and create a new deployment

00:02:29.970 --> 00:02:31.830
based on this version.

00:02:31.830 --> 00:02:34.560
We'll call this deployment Test.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:37.530
This test deployment will
have a different deployment ID

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:40.520
and deployment URL compared
to your head deployment.

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.810
After your colleagues
try out your connector,

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.780
they give you some feedback
and you update your code.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.990
This new version of the code
is now called Test Candidate 2.

00:02:49.990 --> 00:02:52.200
Now, you want to
integrate these changes

00:02:52.200 --> 00:02:54.300
into the test deployment
so your colleagues can

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:55.870
view the changes.

00:02:55.870 --> 00:02:58.230
You don't have to create
a new deployment here.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:00.570
You can just edit the
existing test deployment

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.840
and select the new version
of the code to use.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.180
Having multiple
deployments and the ability

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.580
to update the version
of the code associated

00:03:08.580 --> 00:03:11.550
with each deployment
helps you to have

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.640
a streamlined
deployment process.

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:17.280
We strongly recommend that
you use the head deployment

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:19.560
only during code development.

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.290
Use separate test and
production deployments

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:25.170
for testing and distribution
of your connector.

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:28.260
There are a few things to
keep in mind for deployments.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:31.470
First, connectors in Data
Studio are associated

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.000
with unique deployment links.

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.380
So if you create two deployments
from your connector code

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.420
and add them to
Data Studio, they

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:43.110
will appear as two
separate connectors.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.840
Second, you can edit
and update the name

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.870
for the deployment and the
version of the code associated

00:03:48.870 --> 00:03:50.100
with the connector.

00:03:50.100 --> 00:03:54.210
But you cannot edit a
deployment ID or link.

00:03:54.210 --> 00:03:57.240
And third, if you delete a
deployment from Apps Script,

00:03:57.240 --> 00:03:59.250
it cannot be recovered.

00:03:59.250 --> 00:04:01.920
Let's say your users are
using a specific deployment

00:04:01.920 --> 00:04:04.380
and have created
dashboards from it.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:06.960
If you delete the
deployment from Apps Script,

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.160
the connector and the associated
dashboards will stop working.

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:13.830
To learn more about
deployments and versions,

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:16.480
check out the links in
the description below.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:18.750
And don't forget to
subscribe to the GCP channel

00:04:18.750 --> 00:04:21.157
for more great content
on Data Studio.

00:04:21.157 --> 00:04:21.990
Thanks for watching.

00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:24.630
And until next time, keep
building awesome dashboards.

00:04:24.630 --> 00:04:28.688
[MUSIC PLAYING]

